## Invoice revisited
### Observation
- Found Invoice numbers fall into more than just "Normal" and "C" (Cancellation) — also 
  discovered prefix "A" (Adjustment/bad debt entries)
- Needed a systematic way to confirm no other prefixes existed beyond these three, rather 
  than assuming after finding just two

### Action taken
- Added `Invoice_Prefix` helper column to extract the leading character (or "Numeric" if 
  purely numeric):
  `=IF(ISNUMBER(VALUE(LEFT([@Invoice],1))),"Numeric",LEFT([@Invoice],1))`
- Applied formula across full dataset, confirmed only three categories exist: Numeric, "C", "A"
- Built final categorization column `Sale_or_Not` (values: Normal Sale / Cancellation / 
  Adjustment) to replace ad hoc single-letter checks

### Finding
- "A" (Adjustment) invoices: 3 rows total — all tied to StockCode "B" (Bad debt adjustment, 
  non-product), no CustomerID, negative Price
- "C" (Cancellation) invoices: negative Quantity in nearly all cases (1 exception previously 
  investigated and excluded as a non-product Manual entry)

### Decision
- All "A" and non-product "C"/Manual-type rows will be included in the upcoming consolidated 
  exclusion pass, categorized clearly by `Sale_or_Not` and `Is_Product` for transparent 
  filtering during analysis
                                                                                                                                            
### Investigating Country column
#### Checks performed
- Confirmed no blank Country values
- Built a PivotTable (Rows: Country, Values: Count) to surface every unique value at once, 
  rather than relying on manual scrolling
- Reviewed the full list for spelling inconsistencies, case mismatches, and trailing-space 
  duplicates
- Investigated "Channel Islands" entry — confirmed this is a legitimate, distinct territory 
  (British Crown Dependency, separate from UK/France for shipping/customs purposes), not a 
  data error — retained as-is
- Confirmed "EIRE" is the dataset's legitimate label for Ireland — retained as-is, not a typo

#### Discrepancy found and resolved
- Initial count of "Unspecified" Country using `SUBTOTAL(COUNT, ...)` returned 306, but 
  PivotTable count returned 310
- Cause: SUBTOTAL only counts visible (non-filtered) rows, and a stray filter was active 
  elsewhere in the table at the time, hiding 4 rows from the SUBTOTAL calculation. PivotTable 
  counts from the full underlying source regardless of filter state, giving the correct total
- Takeaway: PivotTable counts are more reliable than SUBTOTAL when filter state isn't 
  explicitly verified beforehand

#### Decision
- "Unspecified" (310 rows) treated as a legitimate placeholder category (deliberately entered, 
  not a missing/blank value) — retained in the cleaned dataset, not excluded
- No spelling/case standardization needed after review — no inconsistent duplicates found 
  requiring a lookup table correction

### Duplicate Invoice Line Items

#### Approach
- Duplicate check needed to be Invoice + StockCode combined (not Invoice alone), since one 
  invoice legitimately has multiple product lines
- Attempted in-table COUNTIF formula, ran into persistent errors — switched to Power Query

#### Steps
- Created `Duplicate_Check` query: grouped by Invoice + StockCode, counted rows as `Dup_Count`
- Merged `Dup_Count` back into main `Table1` query (Left Outer join on Invoice + StockCode), 
  expanded to bring the count into every row

#### Next step
- Filter `Dup_Count > 1` to inspect actual duplicate line items (compare Quantity/Price/Date) 
  and decide true-duplicate vs. legitimate repeat, case by case

#### Finding
- Filtered to Dup_Count > 1 and Fully_Identical = TRUE — zero results
- No exact/redundant duplicate line items exist in the dataset — all Invoice+StockCode 
  groups with multiple entries differ in at least one of Quantity, Price, or InvoiceDate

#### Decision
- No rows removed. All duplicate-looking Invoice+StockCode combinations represent 
  legitimately separate transactions (e.g., repriced items, corrections, or distinct 
  events sharing the same invoice/product) — not data entry errors
- No further action needed on duplicate invoices

